<h2>The Low Center of Gravity Advantage</h2>
<p>At 5'7", Messi's center of gravity — the point around which his body rotates during movement — is significantly closer to the ground than a player of 6'2". This produces three measurable advantages that his height number does not capture.</p>
<h3>Direction Change at Speed</h3>
<p>Changing direction while running requires decelerating, redirecting, and reaccelerating. The physics of this process are more efficient for a lower center of gravity — there is less rotational inertia to overcome and the body is more inherently stable when changing plane. Messi can execute a sharp cut at 18 to 19 mph that a 6-foot defender moving at the same speed cannot mirror without losing balance. This is not a skill so much as a geometry advantage embedded in his body type.</p>
<h3>Ball Retention Under Pressure</h3>
<p>His low build means defenders cannot easily reach the ball with their feet while maintaining a legal challenge position. A defender who is 6 inches taller and trying to win the ball from Messi has to bend significantly to reach it — which puts them off-balance at the moment of contact. Messi's shielding ability is partially a function of his proportions making it mechanically difficult for longer-limbed players to cleanly dispossess him.</p>
<h3>Balance Through Contact</h3>
<p>A lower center of gravity makes maintaining balance through contact easier. This is why Messi draws more fouls than almost any other player despite not being the most physically imposing target — he stays upright through challenges that knock taller players off the ball, which either produces a dispossession or a foul because the defender continues applying pressure expecting him to fall.</p>
<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<h2>Messi at 38 — What Age Does to Athletic Measurements</h2>
<p>Messi turning 38 during this tournament is the headline attached to every piece of coverage, and reasonably so — aging is the one force that has consistently eroded elite athleticism throughout sports history. The relevant question is what specifically has changed and what has not.</p>
