<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How tall is Lionel Messi?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Lionel Messi stands 5 feet 7 inches tall (170 cm) and weighs approximately 159 pounds. He is shorter than the average elite soccer forward, shorter than most players he faces, and shorter than Cristiano Ronaldo by 7 inches. His compact frame gives him a low center of gravity that is a genuine physical advantage for his style of play rather than a limitation he has overcome.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How fast is Messi in mph?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Messi's recorded top speed is approximately 32.5 to 33 km/h, which converts to roughly 20.2 to 20.5 mph. This is fast — well above the average adult and in the elite athlete tier — but not among the fastest at the 2026 World Cup. Haaland (22.7 mph), Mbappé (23.6 mph career best), and several others exceed it. What separates Messi is first-step acceleration, not top-end speed. Use our <a href="https://athletecalculator.com/sprint-speed-calculator">Sprint Speed Calculator</a> to see how your speed compares, and our <a href="https://athletecalculator.com/how-fast-are-world-cup-players">World Cup speed guide</a> for full tournament comparisons.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is Messi's vertical jump?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Messi's vertical jump is estimated at approximately 30 inches based on performance tracking data. This is above the average adult male and demonstrates solid lower-body explosiveness, though it is below elite athletic marks seen at the NBA or NFL combine. Vertical jump is not a primary factor in soccer performance at his position, where horizontal acceleration and close control matter far more. Use our <a href="https://athletecalculator.com/vertical-jump-percentile">Vertical Jump Calculator</a> to see where 30 inches ranks.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How old is Messi at the 2026 World Cup?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Lionel Messi was born on June 24, 1987 — making him exactly 38 years old as the 2026 World Cup group stage concluded. He is the oldest player to score a World Cup hat-trick in the modern era. His hat-trick against Algeria marked his first ever across six World Cup appearances and 23 years of international competition.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How does Messi's height compare to other World Cup stars?</div>
<div class="faq-a">At 5'7", Messi is the shortest of the major 2026 World Cup stars. Haaland is 6'4" (9 inches taller), Ronaldo is 6'2" (7 inches taller), Bellingham is 6'1" (6 inches taller), Mbappé is 5'10" (3 inches taller), and Vinicius is 5'9" (2 inches taller). Despite this, Messi has more World Cup goals, more Ballon d'Or awards, and more trophies than any of them. His height has defined his playing style but never limited his output.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Is Messi faster than Ronaldo?</div>
<div class="faq-a">At their respective peaks, Ronaldo was measurably faster — his career top speed was approximately 38.6 km/h (24.0 mph) against Messi's 32.5 to 33 km/h (20.2 mph). In 2026, both players are in their late 30s to early 40s and have lost several mph from peak speed. Messi at 38 and Ronaldo at 41 are running at roughly similar top speeds during this tournament. Both are playing a more positional game than in their prime years, using anticipation rather than raw speed to create separation.</div>
</div>
